        {
          "at": "2026-08-02T04:15:35Z",
          "findings": [
            {
              "category": "adapter_correctness",
              "file": "libs/infrastructure/src/tddd/signal_evaluator_v2/structural_eq.rs",
              "line": 48,
              "message": "Type aliases still use the name-independent, normalized `generics_structurally_equal`. It canonicalizes parameter names and sorts bounds, so lexically different declarations can compare equal—for example `<T: Clone, U: Clone> = (T, U)` versus `<U: Clone, T: Clone> = (T, U)`, or `T: Clone + Send` versus `T: Send + Clone`. The added tests avoid these counterexamples. Use an alias-specific comparison that preserves parameter order, names, and bound spelling as required by CN-03.",
              "severity": "P0"
            },
            {
              "category": "non_fail_closed_input",
              "file": "libs/infrastructure/src/tddd/catalogue_to_extended_crate_codec/encoder_state_type_codec.rs",
              "line": 288,
              "message": "The JSON DTO accepts both entry-level `generics` and alias-kind `generics`, but this selection silently discards the entry-level declaration whenever the alias-kind list is non-empty, while still applying entry-level `where_predicates`. A document containing conflicting declarations therefore decodes and round-trips but chain-③ evaluates only one of them. Reject conflicting dual declarations at the boundary or represent a single authoritative source.",
              "severity": "P0"
            },
            {
              "category": "error_handling",
              "file": "libs/infrastructure/src/tddd/catalogue_document_codec/encode.rs",
              "line": 251,
              "message": "Decode rejects duplicate alias generic names, but encode copies the public `Vec<MethodGenericParam>` without the equivalent validation. Consequently `CatalogueDocumentCodec::encode` can successfully emit JSON that the same codec refuses to decode. Make alias conversion fallible and return `InvalidEntry`, or enforce uniqueness in a validated domain collection.",
              "severity": "P1"
            }
          ],
          "hash": "rvw1:sha256:91e8bb52bd7e9f75f722e800715cf8cc4ca1289d3f1fb454aa1d861ddb46e899",
          "type": "final",
          "verdict": "findings_remain"
        },
        {
          "at": "2026-08-02T04:50:13Z",
          "findings": [
            {
              "category": "structural equality correctness",
              "file": "libs/infrastructure/src/tddd/signal_evaluator_v2/structural_eq.rs",
              "line": 273,
              "message": "Alias bound comparison strips qualified trait paths to their final segment (`foo::Trait` and `bar::Trait` both become `Trait`). This can mark semantically/lexically different alias declarations as equal, contrary to the required lexical bound comparison; preserve the full bound spelling (including qualified paths and nested argument paths).",
              "severity": "P0"
            }
          ],
          "hash": "rvw1:sha256:89ad0f3595a8a136e748b37d77644d879086db18fbaf3da4e6d49abc9ca900b7",
          "type": "fast",
          "verdict": "findings_remain"
        },
        {
          "at": "2026-08-02T04:55:59Z",
          "findings": [
            {
              "category": "structural equality correctness",
              "file": "libs/infrastructure/src/tddd/signal_evaluator_v2/structural_eq.rs",
              "line": 279,
              "message": "Alias bound comparison still formats generic arguments with the shared short-name formatter. Thus bounds such as `Trait<foo::Arg>` and `Trait<bar::Arg>` can compare equal because nested paths are reduced to `Arg`, violating lexical bound comparison.",
              "severity": "P0"
            },
            {
              "category": "structural equality correctness",
              "file": "libs/infrastructure/src/tddd/signal_evaluator_v2/structural_eq.rs",
              "line": 258,
              "message": "Non-parameter alias where-predicates are compared via `Debug`, which includes rustdoc path IDs. The A-side and implementation-side graphs use different IDs, so equivalent predicates containing resolved paths can compare unequal and produce false mismatches.",
              "severity": "P0"
            }
          ],
